Correct: your code correctly computes the running sum of altitude gains and tracks the maximum altitude reached, starting from a baseline of zero.
2. REVIEW MY CODE

* What I did well: You correctly initialized your highest altitude tracker at 0 to account for the starting point before any bike ride begins. You also correctly maintained a running sum to track the altitude at each stop.
* Is it correct? Yes, your code is fully correct and passes all test cases on LeetCode. For example, if gain is [-5, 1, 5, 0, -7], your code starts at 0, goes down to -5, then -4, then up to 1, then stays at 1, and finally drops to -6, correctly returning 1.
* Logical mistakes, and any edge cases my code misses: There are no logical mistakes. You properly handle edge cases like all negative gains where the biker never goes above the starting altitude of 0 because highest stays at 0.
* Time and space complexity of MY code, each with a one-line reason: Time complexity is O(n) because you loop through the gain array of size n exactly once. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space because you only use two scalar integer variables.
* Why my approach works, or why it doesn't: Imagine you are climbing hills. Each number in the gain array tells you how much higher or lower you go compared to the previous spot. By keeping a running total of your steps and comparing each new total against your highest record, you are guaranteed to find the peak altitude.

3. ALL APPROACHES, FROM BRUTE FORCE TO MOST OPTIMISED
Approach 1: Brute Force with Nested Loops

* Where it comes from: Naive baseline where every position recalculates the altitude from the beginning.
* Idea: For every index i, start an inner loop from 0 up to i and add up all the gain numbers from scratch to find the altitude at that point, then find the maximum among all points.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestAltitude(vector<int>& gain) {
        int highest = 0;
        int n = gain.size();
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) {
            int current_altitude = 0;
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) {
                current_altitude += gain[j];
            }
            if (current_altitude > highest) {
                highest = current_altitude;
            }
        }
        return highest;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n^2) because the inner loop recalculates sums repeatedly. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better: Re-adding numbers from the start at every step is redundant. We can keep a running total in a single pass.

Approach 2: Single-Pass Running Sum (Your Approach - Optimal)

* Where it comes from: Official LeetCode editorial approach and standard prefix sum pattern.
* Idea: Keep a running sum of the altitudes as you iterate through the gain array once, updating your maximum altitude whenever the current sum exceeds it.
* C++ code:

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int largestAltitude(vector<int>& gain) {
        int highest = 0;
        int sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < gain.size(); i++) {
            sum += gain[i];
            if (sum > highest) {
                highest = sum;
            }
        }
        return highest;
    }
};

```

* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) for a single linear scan. Space complexity is O(1) auxiliary space.
* What makes the next approach better: While your approach is already optimal in time and space, we can make the C++ code cleaner and more modern by using range-based loops and std::max.

Approach 3: Modern C++ Range-Based Loop with std::max (Most Optimised Style)

* Where it comes from: Idiomatic modern C++ best practices in LeetCode discussion forums.
* Idea: Use a range-based for loop to visit each gain, update the running altitude sum, and use std::max to cleanly update the highest altitude without manual if-statements.
* C++ code: See section 6.
* Time and space complexity: Time complexity is O(n) and space complexity is O(1).

4. WHERE I STAND

* Which approach did I use? You used Approach 2, the single-pass running sum method.
* Which approach does an interviewer expect for this problem? Interviewers expect Approach 2 or Approach 3. They want to see that you recognize this as a prefix sum problem and avoid the O(n^2) brute force approach.
* Common follow-up questions interviewers ask on this problem, and how to answer them:
* Question 1: What if we also need to return the index where the highest altitude was reached?
* Answer: Keep a variable highest_index initialized to 0. Whenever sum > highest, update highest = sum and highest_index = i + 1 (since index 0 is the starting altitude).
* Question 2: Can the running sum overflow a 32-bit signed integer?
* Answer: LeetCode constraints state that gain elements are between -100 and 100, and the array length is up to 100. The maximum possible altitude is 100 * 100 = 10,000, which easily fits within a standard 32-bit integer, so overflow is impossible here.

5. WHAT I SHOULD REMEMBER (for my revision notes)

* The key insight, in one sentence: The altitude at any point is simply the running sum of all previous gain values, and finding the peak is just tracking the maximum value of that running sum alongside a starting baseline of zero.
* My mistakes, as a short checklist for next time: Your code is completely correct and optimal. Just remember to initialize your peak tracker to 0 to account for the starting altitude before any gains are applied.
* The pattern or idea this question is really about: Prefix Sum and Running Accumulation.

